mclark@c235.globalvision.net (Mitchell Clark) wrote:
>
>
>PURE SPECULATION  --- If I were a Japanese company with "deep pockets"
>and were watching my net worth fluctuate due to uncontrollable events
>in the world money markets I would be tempted to heavily invest in an
>existing company -  if the price was attractive.
>
>FACT --- The value of Digital (in almost every way) is higher than the
>current value of the stock.
>
>QUESTION ---  As DEC stock approaches the mid to low 20�s (on the way
>to the high teens) am I the only one who thinks DEC may be a takeover
>target?
>

You're probably not the only one, but I think the liklihood of a hostile 
takeover is pretty slim. DEC has been buying back stock, and is likely to 
continue to do so. I believe they implemented some sort of "poison pill" 
back in '94 when the stock **did** hit the high teens.

I'm not sure how comfortable the US Government would be with the prospect 
of DEC's advanced technology falling into the hands of a foreign power.

On the matter of a hypothetical takeover by a domestic firm, let's look at 
a couple of the usual suspects. First, of course, is Microsoft. But 
Microsoft already has what it wants: access to Digital's MCS and a foot in 
the door in enterprise accounts. Gates doesn't want to get into the 
service business; why spend money acquiring a business that generates only 
half the gross margin of software?

Aaron Goldberg wrote an interesting editorial in PC Week last summer 
regarding a hypothetical Digital/Compaq merger. Compaq wants to be an 
enterprise player, and DEC's presence in this arena--not to mention the 
firm's service prowess--would be beneficial to Compaq. Compaq has in the 
past evaluated RISC architectures as "Intel product line extenders," which 
happens to be the way Digital currently pitches Alpha. And of course, 
both firms are WNT-centric (Compaq more so than DEC).





