tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at (Thomas Leitner) writes:

> Next monday get get a test alpha system with an ATI Mach 64 graphics
> board and want to run it under Digital Unix. In the long run I want to
> use a DEC VRT19-HA fixed frequency monitor with the system.

There is a sync-on-green FAQ you probably will want to take a look at. 

> The monitor has only three BNC connectors while the ATI Mach 64 has a
> standard PC SVGA connector and I hope to get an appropriate adapter
> cable.

You need to be a bit handy with a soldering iron, it's a bit more
complicated than just going from one plug to another, but not overly
so.   See the above mentioned FAQ.

> 1.) Can the ATI Mach 64 be configured (by some console command) to
>     deliver the sync signals in green as the VRT-19HA monitor
>     requires it? 

It's all in the converter, AFAIK.

> I know that the DEC TGA card can be configured like
> this.

You sure?  Do you have any more information on this?

> 2.) Can the ATI Mach 64 be configured to stay in 1280x1024 @ 72Hz
>     in all modes (text mode as well as graphics mode). Can this
>     configuration be done in the Alpha or would I need to plug
>     the board into a PC and run the configuration there?

I don't know about Dig. Unix but under Linux/XFree, you have complete
control over the clock frequencies under X, and should be able to
deliver the syncs the monitor requires.  With SVGAtextmode, you can
probably achieve what you want in text mode as well.

You can possibly run XFree under DU as well, or alternatively configure
the vendor supplied X similarly, but I wouldn't know about that. 

~kzm




