In article <55ak3j$i3q$1@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>, everhart@star.enet.dec.com (Glenn C. Everhart) writes:

:I remember an announcement some time ago of prelim manuals for converting
:step 1 to step 2 drivers and so on on the net..

   The driver conversion manuals are in the Alpha Driver Developer
   portion of the OpenVMS documentation set -- there are several
   different ways one can order the documentation, and it can get
   somewhat confusing -- particularly if you haven't seen the Guide
   to the Documentation Set manual.  (These manuals first appeared
   around V6.1 -- which is when the Step 2 support was integrated.)

:As for some of the hardware manuals, I don't have any special info. Note
:there are a bunch of .digital.com websites around though
(service.digital.com... and many more). It might not hurt to see if,
:say, Altavista has any info it can help find.

   I'll pass along a request to the writers to determine if we can
   reasonably include platform-related documentation references in
   in the OpenVMS device driver manuals.  (A table containing a
   selection of various documentation part numbers...)

:My experience going step1 to step2 is that the changes can mostly be
:made rather mechanically, but the more complex the device the harder
:the changes in the interface can be.

   Most of the cases I've seen are fairly simple and straightforward.
   The lower-level requirements haven't changed that much.  There are
   options -- such as the availability of a C calling interface -- that
   have been added.  There are usually some changes needed, and exactly
   what changes are needed depends on what the driver is doing...

:As for "cpu" manuals, you must remember that there's a separate part
:of the code called "cpu code" here and sometimes people drift into
:their own everyday-used jargon. The "cpu code" is different for every
:platform basically and implements very low level interfaces so that
:everything else (pretty much) does not have to worry about the differences.

   The technical manuals for the CPU are required only if you are
   doing very low-level privileged-mode activities such as direct
   access to specific areas of physical memory, or need to know
   specific details of how physical memory is configured, or need
   to deal with such things as (using the KA630 module as a random
   example) the CPU CSRs, etc.

:Different sites use different words for this kind of stuff, and in general
:the "cpu code" (as the term is used here) sits below device drivers
:logically and is used by them a fair bit just to avoid maintenance logistical
:problems.

   Glenn is correct that the low-level hardware interface is often
   well below the level of drivers doing `fairly standard driver
   stuff' -- reading and writing to Q-bus or PCI widgets, etc., are
   all available and documented in the driver manuals.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
