  Dunno, try Barnes & Noble.  They are public/industry busses
  (ISA, EISA, PCI) - not DEC proprietary.  The documentation I
  have is in the form of technical specifications from outside
  companies on these busses - I don't have any DEC documentation
  on the busses, except for the bus interface chips.


> > So.  I repeat.  A *device driver* writer should not need the technical docs
> > on the specific *platform*.  Unless you are doing something unusual, or are
> > in fact writing platform support.
> 
> OK, accept that I am doing something unusual.   Or accept that
> I want to do something platform-specific.   Or accept that, despite
> DEC's best intentions, I *do* need information provided in the
> User's Manual.   Or accept that I simply want to know what goes
> on "under the hood".   Now, for the umpteenth time...
> 
> WHAT ARE THE (valid) PART NUMBERS FOR THE TECHNICAL MANUALS????
> 
> Why are these manuals being kept secret?
> 


  Frankly, because except for the Hudson evaluation boards, I'm not even
  sure that such things are even still published.  I certainly have never
  seen one.  We (the base O/S) are provided with a technical specification
  from the platform group.  This specification is used by the systems and
  bus groups to do the platform-specific implementation (SYS$CPU_ROUTINES).
  Things that vary from platform-to-platform we try to hide the user from
  by providing vectored IOC$... (etc) routines for.  And we provide user
  documentation for writing a device driver that is bus and device dependent,
  but platform independent.

  So.  I never see a formal manual, if such a thing exists.  I can go off
  and ask the HW folks if they actually publish the technical specifications
  externally (and I will).  I don't think that anything is being kept secret.


_Fred



