Alan Frisbie wrote:
> 
> In article <54le99$is6@usenet.pa.dec.com>,
> hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com.no-spam (Stephen Hoff Hoffman) writes:
> 
> > :Here we have two people from DEC giving opposing answers.
> > :Is it any wonder that customers are frustrated with DEC?
> >
> >   I don't see a conflict in the above answers -- both Fred and I were
> >   clearly refering to the folks that are writing drivers and low-level
> >   code when refering to the technical documentation, and thus need the
> >   documentation associated with this task.
> 
> Go back and read my posting again.   Fred said:
>
> ...


  Egads.  Let's try this again.

  In the past, you might have found a technical manual on say, the MicroVAX II.
  And you might indeed have needed this manual in addition to the manuals on
  the device adapters, the Qbus, and the device driver manuals to develop a
  device driver.

  The Alpha platforms today use standard peripheral busses, that we document
  for generic user developed drivers:

	- PCI
	- ISA
	- EISA

  The way that device drivers are written for these busses is platform
  independent (or at least it should be 99.99% of the time).  And there are
  standard system interfaces to allow device drivers to map their devices, and
  to get information and resources.  Heck, in V7.1 there is even a standard
  way to *autoconfigure* the devices (which was one of the things that had
  been platform/bus specific.

  You *will* need the technical manual for the DEVICE that you are trying to
  program.  You *may* want the *BUS* technical manual.  And you *will* need
  the book I referenced (Writing OpenVMS Alpha Device Drivers in C) to make
  life a *lot* easier.  Appendix A gives about the nly interesting tidbit of
  *platform* specific information that a device driver writer should need -
  the address maps for specific systems.  And you can round things out with
  the standard OpenVMS device driver reference manual.

  I will note that the information on autoconfiguration - including how to
  write an ICBM - is out of date as of V7.1, since most drivers can now be
  autoconfigured using a standard ASCII text file edit (this is how all VMS
  device drivers are configured in V7.1).  And that the ISA_CONFIG.DAT method
  of ISA configuration will be dropped in a future release due to the new
  common autoconfiguration method in V7.1.

  So.  I repeat.  A *device driver* writer should not need the technical docs
  on the specific *platform*.  Unless you are doing something unusual, or are
  in fact writing platform support.

  I do this (device drivers, platform support, and I designed and implemented the
  new autoconfiguration support) for a living.  And I am the OpenVMS Systems
  Technical Leader... so I do hope you can take my word for it ;-)

_Fred



