  We have an alpha 3000/500 in use as a student email machine.
It has on the order of 8000 accounts, most inactive.  It is
also running C2 security stuff.  It also has lsm running on 
the file systems which store the home directories.
  Unloaded, it was a pretty perky machine, however we have been
receiving complaints of it being pokey.  Our research into the
complaints was that pine was taking too long to load.
  Indeed, sometimes it takes 30 seconds for pine to load.  The
mail files are stored in people's home directories.  Sometimes
it would load instantly (I have six messages each of which is 
very small) sometimes it would take a really long time.
  At first, I could not figure out why it took such a wide range
of times to load.  Then, I discovered that by running a simple
shell script, I could reliably cause it to take 30 seconds to a
minute.  This is the script:
while :; do ls -l /home; done;
  This takes about a minute to complete, and while it is running
pine takes forever to start.  While running top, I noticed that the 
automounter is using up 90% of the CPU while this script is 
running.  Why is the automounter affected by this script?  I have
tried running other programs which take up as much CPU as
possible, none of them affect the load times of pine in the same
way as the automounter/this script.
