In article <550e2q$gr2@Mars.mcs.net>, fluke@MCS.COM (Greg Linder) writes:
|	I have a MicroVAX II machine which I have recently re-constructed and
|installed VMS 5.5 onto. Now I am trying to wrestle this machine into a
|VAXCluster relationship with a VAXstation 3100 I also possess. I have the
|VAXStation's end working fine, according to the lights on my AUI adapter, but
|the MicroVAX II's Exos Exerlan card refuses to be even recognized by VMS as 
|a network device!
|	Does this Exerlan card even support VAXcluster? Is there any drivers
|or anything like that needed to make this card show up to VMS? I have an old
|M7504, which doesn't function above VMS 5.x for VAXcluster, so I've been told,
|or else I would try to VAXCluster with this.
|	Any thoughts?

No, the Excelan card does NOT support SCS (VAXcluster), nor DECnet,
nor any protocols other than TCP/IP.  And I don't think there's
any supported product to run TCP/IP over it any more, either.

You would need a special driver to get this card to even talk
to VMS - presumably from an old EXOS distribution floppy.  If you
had one that worked with VMS V5.5, then you *might* be able to
get talking TCP/IP if you also had MultiNet installed (though
MultiNet no longer supports these cards.)

The best thing for you to do here would be to pitch this card
and get a used real Digital Ethernet adapter - a DELQA.  Stay
away from the DEQNA, which should be even cheaper, but is not
supported by VMS V5.5 for SCS or DECnet (though you *could*
downgrade to V5.4 and use the DEQNA.)

