Harald Schoenfeld <hs@undine.physik.uni-erlangen.de> wrote:

>Hi,

>does anybody know, if the standard C++ compiler from decsupports
>the STL (Standard template library) and the rather new RTTI (Runtime
>type checking) features?

As I see no other responses (which may well be the fault of my news
server) I will attempt a reply.  Note that my experience is with the
OpenVMS platform.  However, the compiler and standard library
situation should be very similar on Digital Unix.  As it has been a
few days, I've CC'd your mailbox.

The short answer is yes (to STL) and no (to RTTI).

A slightly longer answer is that DEC C++ started supplying the STL
with version 5.3 of the compiler.  It is based on the HP
implementation but has been modified to conform to the ANSI/ISO Draft
Working Paper (DWP).  It is thread safe but not exception safe (the HP
reference implementation is neither).

Digital recently announced that they have been liscenced to provide
Rogue Wave's implementation of the standard library.  I've heard that
version will start shipping with version 5.6, with a more complete
implementation to ship with 6.0.

The next "major" release of the compiler (I assume this will be
version 6.0) is expected to conform to the ANSI/ISO DWP (exactly which
version I don't know - probably some 1996 revision).  The current
compiler is based on the ARM, with several features from the DWP.  I
would expect 6.0 to have RTTI.  I have no idea what the expected
delivery date is for 6.0.

Hope this helps.

>Harald

Bruce




