[Robert Rodgers]

|   >What???  6 MB/s??? SCSI can go up to 40 MB/s with conventional controllers
|   >(Ultra-Wide) and 100 MB/s with Fiber Channel.  I've never seen EIDE over
|   >~16 MB/s.
|   
|   'Course, if you want disks that actually deliver even 1/10th that,
|   even in short bursts, you might have some difficulty.

1/10 of 100 MByte/s in bursts is doable, given the right equipment. 1/10 of
40 Mbyte/s *sustained* is easy.

I can get more than 5 MByte/s *sustained* with a fairly inexpensive SCSI
controller and disk. The same can be done with EIDE.

Here is a quick test I ran just now - reading 500 MByte from the raw device
using lmdd:

# ./lmdd if=/dev/rsd0c of=internal bs=512k count=1000
500.00 MB in 95.79 secs, 5345.18 KB/sec

This is on a Pentium 133 running FreeBSD, with the ASUS PCI-SC200 controller
(Fast/Narrow SCSI) and an IBM DORS-32160 2.16 GB SCSI disk.

And an EIDE example:

# ./lmdd if=/dev/rwd0c of=internal bs=512k count=1000
500.00 MB in 75.84 secs, 6751.05 KB/sec

This is on a Pentium Pro 200 running FreeBSD, and a Quantum Fireball 1.7 GB
EIDE disk. (FreeBSD uses PIO, not DMA.)

With the possible exception of the PPro box, none of this equipment can be
called expensive.

Steinar Haug, Nethelp consulting, sthaug@nethelp.no



