Scott Sanbeg wrote:
> My company has been granted ownership of several DEC pieces, including
> some Alpha 2100 4/200's.  And I am trying to identify what it is that I
> don't already know (furthermore, the original owners made off with the
> majority of the docs).
> 
> There is a FDDI card in the computer that *looks* like the defea-da NIC
> on one of Digital's web pages.  So I ftp'd over there today, printed out
> defea-om.ps, only to find that the defea-da is not mentioned, nor
> illustrated.  The card, which I saw only momentarily, is told to me to
> have two MIC A/B ports and a RJ-45 port, and it occupies two
> interface-slots in the machine (one motherboard slot, w/a ribbon
> connector connecting the daughter<?> board).  Anyway, I have to get
> configuration information on whatever the card is as soon as possible.

Hi Scott.  You *DO* have a DEFEA-DA adapter.  The DEFEA adapter line has
been rearchitected to get all models down to a single board.  The new
FDDI EISA Dual Attachment Station (DAS) Multimode Fiber (MMF) controller
is a single full-length EISA card using SC transceivers.  It's called
the DEFEA-DB.

The model you have (DEFEA-DA) is the original design which consists of a
full-length EISA card with the A port connection, a shorter daughter
card with the B port connection, and a ribbon cable that connects the
two.  Both A and B ports use the larger ANSI MIC transceivers.  By the
way, the RJ-45 port is for connecting an optional optical bypass relay. 
Functionality-wise, the two cards are the same.  The new DEFEA-DB,
however, only takes one slot, requires a different cable connector, and
uses a green/amber LED instead of the green/red LED on the DEFEA-DA.

I'll email you a PostScript document of the older manual.  Regards.
