I have encountered a perplexing problem on an ALPHA running Digital
UNIX.  I have a large program written mainly in FORTRAN that calls a C
routine to read a large ( 1800000 byte ) file.  When the routine tries
to read the file the process goes into an uninteruptable sleep for as
long as I've cared to wait.  The process cannot be killed and the
machine must be rebooted to remove it.  The problem happens whenever the
program is run.

To debug it I created a minimal program to read the same file using the
same C routine.  This program ALMOST always runs fine.  Occasionally it
hangs in the same manner as the original program.

This code is being ported from SunOS where it runs fine. 

Any help would be greatly appreciated.



