> I admit to being well versed on Intel Platforms, but in the near
> future I will be entering the Dgitial world with the delivery of a
> relatively new (less than 5 months old) DEC Alpha Workstation (I
> believe that it is a 255/300 but am not sure).  It is being released
> to me at the end of a development project.  I know the following:
> 2 1.2 Gbyte Hard Disks
> 32 Mbyte Ram
> Video Controller (no idea what model/configuration)
> It should be arrving with Open VMS installed (hardly the ideal OS for
> SOHO useage! :-)   )

Agreed.

> My forecasted useage would be as file server/workstation on a small
> network.  Am thinking about Windows NT.  Any comments?
> I know that I would have to purchase the OS from digital,

On the contrary, *any* Windows NT cd (server or workstation) will have the
Alpha version on it.  Look in the \Alpha directory.

> but what
> about MSOffice?  Can I use the Intel version of Office 95/97 or am I
> going to be going back to Digital again?  If so is there an upgrade
> policy vis a vis my old copy of MS Office for the Intel platform,

I don't know of a native Alpha NT version of MS Office, but DEC has a
utility called FX!32 ($free) that will let you run any Intel NT software on
Alpha NT.  Microsoft also has a utility in beta to do the same thing (wx86
or something like that).  Digital's utility actually translates the intel
code and creates .exes and .dlls with Alpha code to run.  So, it's very
slow the first time you run it (it's translating), but after that, it's at
least 75% the speed of a true, native Alpha app.

> How about Linux?

I love Linux, but I don't use any flavor of UNIX for file and print
services.  I can only think of ONE advantage that NT has over UNIX, and
thats file and print services.  I have Alphas running NT (file/print
server), Digital UNIX (web/database servers), and Linux (workstations) and
I like them all.

> Any other suggestions?

If you're a UNIX guru, I'd run Linux.  Digital UNIX will cost you about
$1500 for a workstation license, so that's probably out.  Otherwise, go NT.

> Offers?

No offers you'd seriously consider.  Alphas are damn expensive.  That's why
many of mine (mosty workstations) are Alpha clones - about 1/4 the cost of
a DEC Alpha.  The clones still have a Digital-made chip and motherboard,
the vendor purchases those from DEC, then assembles the machine.  E.g.: I
just purchased an Alpha clone - the exact replica of an AlphaStation
500/333.  I purchased the machine for $6,028 with 2GB UW SCSI-2, 128MB RAM,
2MB cache.  I got a quote on the identical machine from DEC - are you
ready? - $23,500!

'Nuff said.

> Thanks!!!!
