In article <54ifma$196@enva1.env.uea.ac.uk> zebra@enva1.env.uea.ac.uk (J.S.Brainard) writes:
>We have made the recent discovery that SunOS 4.1.3/4 can't tar -xvf
>tar files created under OSF/1 3.x.  This is a real nuisance since these
>two are the dominant OS's at our site.  Also, I have recently written
>some software I would like to distribute by anon. ftp.  Working mostly
>on alpha platforms I naturally used the alpha to tar my code up --
>but this means SunOS users can't untar it!  And there are an awful lot
>of SunOS users out there.

We had this problem too, although we didn't finmd out until we'd distributed
quite a large number of tapes to people. To fix this we took two measures.

1. Copy the old tar program from OSF/1 V2.x and use this instead.

2. Wrote a converter program. To get a copy see:
	ftp://ftp.mrc-lmb.cam.ac.uk/ftp/pub/staden/src/tarfix-1.2.shar
It's actually a modified copy of another program, but it does the job.

The problem is that the SunOS 4 systems look at the filename to figure out
whether something is a  directory or not. If it is, then it'll have a / on the
end. In addition to this the file type info should also contain the ISDIR flag
set. However SunOS 4 only checks the file name. OSF/1 only adds the ISDIR bit.
Arguably the OSF system is correct - most other systems seem to cope happily
with it.

Anyway, with a conversion program there's no need to recreate all your
existing tars. I suspect gnu tar would work too, which is a 3rd option only if
these tars are for your own local use.

	James
