|> :>  exception of the Evaluation Board kits from Digital Semiconductor.
|> :> 
|> :>  Very little of it would be of use to you unless you were going to
|> :>  write the platform support code - as opposed to a device driver.

ok, I read that last sentence as saying traditional technical doc 
would be of little use to you if you were going to write a device driver,
in Fred's opinion.

and I'd also read it as specifically distingushing between platform support
code and drivers, in that drivers do not need the tech doc and platform
support does.


|> :quoting hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoff Hoffman):
|> :
|> :>   You'll need the Alpha driver manual set, and
|> :>   the CPU technical manual(s) for the particular processor(s) in question,
|> :>   in order to write a driver for your widget.  (The technical manuals will
|> :>   provide you with the necessary adapter information.)

and that pretty clearly says you'll need the CPU technical manuals to write
your device driver, in the author's opinion.


|> :Here we have two people from DEC giving opposing answers.
|> :Is it any wonder that customers are frustrated with DEC?
|> 
|>   I don't see a conflict in the above answers -- both Fred and I were
|>   clearly refering to the folks that are writing drivers and low-level
|>   code when refering to the technical documentation, and thus need the
|>   documentation associated with this task.


I gotta agree with Alan, the two answers he quoted seem to me to conflict.
As explained above, it seems to me that Fred distingushed between drivers 
and other low level code, and disagreed about the need for doc such as 
CPU tech manuals when writing driver code.

The fact that one of the authors quoted interprets them differently than
two outside observers may indicate a deeper problem, and one that may offer
further insights into the roots of customer frustration with DEC.



|> :If DEC no longer provides technical documentation, that begs
|> :the question of why DEC *ever* provided it?   Why did customers
|> :need it then and not now?
|> 
|>   Alan, you, Fred, and I have all been at this long enough to remember
|>   programming before the era of `commercial off the shelf' (`COTS')
|>   products.  (There are folks that don't know the Internet predates
|>   the web. :-)  Program development techniques and tools have changed
|>   over the years, as well -- I wrote my last paper-tape-punch device
|>   driver well over ten years ago.  :-)


Support resources and quality have also changed a lot over the years.  
One thing is still the same: customers who have mission critical systems 
need to be able to depend on vendor support, or to be able to be self-reliant.  
Those are among the issues that drove doc contents in the old days, and those
issues have not disappeared.

Also, there are customers and applications that still do not fit the COTS
model, those situations require doc (and support) beyond the COTS model.  If
you don't satisfy those needs, you lose that business. 

FWIW, I don't know what you guys are doing these days, but I'm typing this on
a PA-RISC desktop that's being used to build our next generation mission critical
apps after Alpha was one of the losers in our evaluations.  Can't say why,
I wasn't part of that decision, but I do know that none of the mission critical
code is COTS...

--bruce mcculley
 (the opinions expressed herein are personal and are not sanctioned by Instinet)



