> >Is there a general feeling that the new Alpha machines or newer
> >versions of OpenVMS are low quality? 

One thing that Apple did right it its own architecture migration was a
SEAMLESS migration. The PPC boxes automatically emulate bits of code
that are written for the older 68k machines.

So, you can have a native PPC main application that uses services that
are written in 68k code SEAMLESSLY. (or vice versa).

When a computer company reaches the limit of the architecture it chose
and must switch architecture, it should not be the customers who pay. In
the case of VAX to ALPHA, it seems that it is the customers who are
paying for DEC's inability/unwillingness to provide a "free" migration.
Not only do customers have to pay, but the migration is also
technologically not "automatic". 

At one point Digital did try to *sell* VEST, didn't it ???????
How many products did Digital decide NOT to port to ALPHA ?

The messaging area is definitely one where Digital lost BIG TIME because
of the migration to alpha. The fact that Message Router was/will not be
migrated to ALPHA and that there was no only a lack of technological
migration, but the replacement products were DIFFERENT PRODUCTS (MAILBUS
400 and X.500 directory) requiring purchase of different licences
alltogether resulted in delayed purchases of ALPHA and by the time ALPHA
became a viable solution for messaging, other "mainstream" products from
other vendors had become popular.

Sorry DEC, while you were concentrating on counting pennies to make sure
your migration didn't cost YOU anything, customers went away because
this migration was just too much hassles. (Combine this with the almost
simultaneous migration to DECnet Phase 5, VAX-C to DEC-C and the endless
downsizing, product maturing, product sell-off and it is no wonder that
customers don't feel secure and comfortable with VMS. And when you add
DEC's marketing afforts to get us out of VMS to NT or UNIX it is no
wonder customers think twice about making the big jump to ALPHA.

Combine this with many VMS releases in this time frame and you get a lot
of confused folks who are still wondering if an application that ran on
VMS will run on oPeNvMs :-) :-) :-) ;-) :-)

I think that VMS moved too quickly and unfortunatly in the same time
frame as Digital's near bankrupcy/downsizing. VMS moved too quickly at a
time where Digital didn't know where it was going, at a time where
Digital didn't know which products to keep, sell or kill. (or revive, in
the case of FMS and DEC Document)



