Daniel Maloney <dmaloney@argonet.co.uk> writes:

>  Torben AEgidius Mogensen wrote:

>> Also, the main cost of parallel systems is not the processors, it is the
>> interconnection. This reduces the advantage of ARM processors. 

>  Er, wasn't that why the idea was to have multiple SA's on on chip?

Interconnection is costly even if on a single chip. The problem is
that the number of connections is very large once you have more than a
few processors and unless you choose a regular grid, the layout of
these on silicon (which can't use 3 dimensions very well) is
problematical and will likely use more space than the CPU's
themselves. And then you will have to add buffers etc. to the
connections. The cost can be reduced somewhat by using shared memory
(as on the Hydra board), but this doesn't scale very well.
Furthermore, you either need snooping caches or a strict policy of
communicating only through uncached locations.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)



