   I will assume you have evaluated other archival storage technologies.

   Most every magtape ever connected to the VAX-11/780 out-draws the
   power requirements of the entire the VAX 4000-300 system by several
   orders of magnitude -- I can only assume there is something very
   special about this particular peripheral.  (I do not recognize the
   `NTDS interface' mentioned in your posting and you do not mention
   a particular model of magtape -- with more information, I might be
   able to offer an alternative.)

   The DLT, DDS and similar storage devices will be far faster, far more
   reliable, and far more capacious than any VAX-11/780-vintage.  If the
   nine-track magtape interchange is a requirement, TSV and TSZ series
   SCSI magnetic tape devices -- and various third-party devices -- are
   readily available, and can be connected to the VAX 4000-300 series.

   The VAX-11/780 backplane was the SBI, and there were SBI adapters for
   Massbus, UNIBUS, and CI. (Among others.)  Most tape drives of that
   vintage were TU7x series drives, and most were connected to a Massbus
   via the VAX-11/780 SBI-Massbus or the VAX-11/750 CMI-Massbus Massbus
   adapter.

