Gee, I just checked the stock price for DEC.  It dropped over $5
per share today, and it's at a 52 week low at 28 3/4.  That's
a drop of $47 per share since March, or a loss of 63%.  In fact
it's the lowest price per share in 2 years.  

One problem I think DEC has is the high cost of it's maintanence
contracts.  They charge too much to maintain older equipment.  It's
cheaper to buy new PC's than it is to maintain old DEC equipment.
Don't get me wrong, though.  The field engineers I've dealt with
are very good, and they all do a great job.  I just think the
monthly costs are too high.

For example, at my old job we had 2 RAID disks enclosures with
25 disk drives.  These were non DEC disks.  They were going to
charge us $25 per month for each disk to add them to our maintanence
agreement.  That's $625 per month for all of these disks.  Most
of them were 1-2gb SCSI disks that cost $600-700 new.  That's
enough money to buy a new disk every month and put it in ourselves.

Another problem is the high cost of DEC replacement parts.  DEC
probably has warehouses of old parts that they can't get rid of
because nobody wants to pay DEC's high prices.  Many of the parts
are propriotary and you can't get them anywhere else.  Probably most
of these spare parts will end up in the dumpster.  Then the
customers will buy PC's and dump the old DEC dinosaurs.  At least
with a PC you can buy off the shelf parts and put them in yourself.
