harley@pauillac.inria.fr (Robert Harley) writes:

>torbenm@diku.dk (Torben AEgidius Mogensen) writes:
>>Daniel Maloney <dmaloney@argonet.co.uk> writes:
>>>Torben AEgidius Mogensen wrote:
>>>>Also, the main cost of parallel systems is not the processors, it is the
>>>>interconnection. This reduces the advantage of ARM processors. 
>>>
>>>  Er, wasn't that why the idea was to have multiple SA's on on chip?
>>
>>Interconnection is costly even if on a single chip. The problem is
>>that the number of connections is very large once you have more than a
>>few processors

>The number of connexions per processor is typically constant or
>logarithmic in the total number of processors.  This doesn't strike me
>as "very large".

>>and unless you choose a regular grid, the layout of
>>these on silicon (which can't use 3 dimensions very well) is
>>problematical

>So choose a regular 2D grid!

A regular 2D grid is very limiting and makes it hard to exploit
parallelism except for a few specialized applications.

>>and will likely use more space than the CPU's themselves.

>I doubt it very much.  Do you have any compelling reason for this opinion?

Even in this case, you have to have 4 connections per processor, each
with a buffer etc. And you still have to route some of these around to
the 'other side' of the processor. If each processing element has
local memory (otherwise it is pretty useless), the length of these
connections will be quite long.


>>The cost can be reduced somewhat by using shared memory
>>(as on the Hydra board), but this doesn't scale very well.

>Which cost?  The main advantage of shared memory seems to be that
>programmers don't like doing message-passing.  They want their
>parallel machine to look somewhat like a sequential machine...

The cost is reduced by having all machines connect to a single bus,
rquiring only a single 2-way connector per processor. and by using a
single global memory instead of local memory (which tends to be hard
to utilize fully).

>PS: T.I. claims 2000 "ARM processors", not StrongARMs by a long shot.

I agree. They were probably thinking of ARM7TDM cores. StrongARM has
2.5 million transistors, 2.2 million of which are in the caches
(source: http://infopad.EECS.Berkeley.EDU/HotChips8/4.2/). So the core
is in the neighbourhood of 300K transistors, which is about the same
as other ARM cores.

Also, 2000 ARM processors aren't useful without a good deal of
interconnect and some local memory. I would much rather have a single
500MHz StrongARM with 16MB of fast on-chip RAM (and an FPU).

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)



