In article <1996Oct21.143523.989@flying-disk.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.com (Alan Frisbie) writes:
:In article <3267722D.6AAB09EF@star.enet.dec.com>, 
:Fred Kleinsorge <kleinsorge@star.enet.dec.com> writes:
:
:>  It is not clear to me that we provide traditional technical
:>  documentation on all of our system platforms.  With perhaps the
:>  exception of the Evaluation Board kits from Digital Semiconductor.
:> 
:>  Very little of it would be of use to you unless you were going to
:>  write the platform support code - as opposed to a device driver.
:> 
:>  The device driver manual is the primary source of information on
:>  developing a device driver for VMS.  
:
:Yet, in article <4v21r4$8ko@usenet.pa.dec.com>, 
:hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoff Hoffman) writes:
:
:>   You'll need the Alpha driver manual set, and
:>   the CPU technical manual(s) for the particular processor(s) in question,
:>   in order to write a driver for your widget.  (The technical manuals will
:>   provide you with the necessary adapter information.)
:
:Here we have two people from DEC giving opposing answers.
:Is it any wonder that customers are frustrated with DEC?

  I don't see a conflict in the above answers -- both Fred and I were
  clearly refering to the folks that are writing drivers and low-level
  code when refering to the technical documentation, and thus need the
  documentation associated with this task.

  Both of us also refered to the requirement to order the technical
  documentation -- that it was no longer shipping with the systems. 
  (Even the ten-year-old MicroVAX II system's EK-KA630-UG technical
  documentation -- I think that was the part number for the _KA630
  CPU User's Guide_ -- had to be ordered seperately from the system.)

:If DEC no longer provides technical documentation, that begs
:the question of why DEC *ever* provided it?   Why did customers
:need it then and not now?

  Alan, you, Fred, and I have all been at this long enough to remember
  programming before the era of `commercial off the shelf' (`COTS')
  products.  (There are folks that don't know the Internet predates
  the web. :-)  Program development techniques and tools have changed
  over the years, as well -- I wrote my last paper-tape-punch device
  driver well over ten years ago.  :-)

  Most of the platforms are now using chipsets that have documentation for
  the internal operations, such as the Alpha microprocessor and the DECchip
  `glue' chips used for memory management, etc.  The technical documentation
  is (still) available and I fully expect it will remain available -- though
  few folks know it (still) exists, and fewer folks need it.

  Some of this technical documentation is available free of charge, or at
  very low cost.

  If one thing has changed in all this, it is that the part numbers are
  getting harder to find.  (I will see if I can get the platform technical
  documentation part numbers -- at least for some of the platforms -- added
  into a future version of the OpenVMS device driver manuals.)
  
  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
