Mr. R. Tierney wrote:

> Stephen is correct in saying...
> > StrongARM is a trademark of ARM LTd, not Digital Semiconductor.

That's quite true but also quite unimportant.  StrongARM is only a name.

> Digital have licensed ARM technology, and applied processes used in Alpha
> production to up-clock ARM's - so making Strong ones ;-)

No, they've done more than that.  The split I/D cache (and I believe
some pipeline tweaks) are not part of the ARM 4 architecture.  This is
where Digital's license differs from the others, they have the right to
make changes to the CPU architecture itself.

> That would mean that any of ARM's semiconductor partners have access to the
> new technology, so we could in fact see ARM, or say Digital themselves
> utilising the technology on 0.18 micron or better systems, as long as they
> licence it for the purpose.

No, the SA-110 belongs to Digital and unless they license their
improvements back to ARM or someone else, we will not see the SA-110
from other manufacturers.  (This is as explained to me by the Digital
FAE).  The remaining ARM partners have access to ARM's designs, which do
not include the SA-110. Things may have changed since May, when I was
told this, but I haven't heard anything to the contrary so far.

For the time being, the ARM810 is the fastest ARM you will find from the
other vendors.

Cheers,
Scott



