[SNIP]

> No, the SA-110 belongs to Digital and unless they license their
> improvements back to ARM or someone else, we will not see the SA-110
> from other manufacturers.  (This is as explained to me by the Digital
> FAE).  The remaining ARM partners have access to ARM's designs, which do
> not include the SA-110. Things may have changed since May, when I was
> told this, but I haven't heard anything to the contrary so far.

Right! Having had a closer look at both ARM's and Digital's related sites, I
have to agree with you on the point that the 'architecture' has been
licenced to Digital to do (virtually) with as they like.

Ther is one point however, it seems that ARM have some sort of agreement
with Digital that any of ARM's technology partners can have access to the
StrongARM designs if they want to licence it (presumably from Digital?).

Digital have agreed that all of ARM's partners can have access through
licences.

ARM want's all of their partners to benefit from any new technological
advancements. Digital have the momentum ATM. TI might have it next year, and
Bell (Not a partner yet but you never know...) or Samsung may have it in two
years time. What one company developes can benefit a whole group of
companies. Eventually, it will come full-circle and every company will
benefit from the best of all technologies. 

Digital will still make substantial profits out of the technology because
they have a special licence (as you say) to sell the StrongARM as 'their
own', as well as providing licences.


Anyway, back to the thread... I would stil like to know if ARM are
interested in doing an MP system on-a-chip?

I realise that the SA1500(FE?) will be fast, and that it will eventually be
tweaked up to about 500MHz, but what comes after that? (Not asking too much
am I? ;-).

Will PDA's and STB's and other embedded systems really need 200-500MHz
processors? Especially ones with DSP and FP? Or are we starting to get into
the realms that the 'peripherals' are getting more powerful than the main
processors?

I would love to see ARM do a main processor - cause I know it would be fast,
cheap and very efficient.

And, if you hadn't guessed, I'm also an enthiusiat having owned ARM's since
the ARM2 back in 1987. The work Digital has done on the ARM architecture has
brought it up to levels where it can (yet again) compete on the Desktop's of
the world.

Is there /really/ no market for very high power, very efficient, very
inexpensive Multi-Processor systems? I just can't comprehend that...

