Mr. R. Tierney wrote:

> Will PDA's and STB's and other embedded systems really need 200-500MHz
> processors? Especially ones with DSP and FP?

Yes and far more.  The Newton's handwriting recognition (and upcoming
voice recognition) is a long way from being either fast enough or good
enough.  Considerably more horsepower will be needed in this area.  Full
motion video with good compression will also require significant
processing.  The goal of PDA manufacturers is perhaps loftier than
desktop PC manufacturers.  They want to create "personal digital
assistants".  My "personal human assistant" is so complex I can't
imagine how long it will take to produce a PDA to match his
capabilities, and he's only 2 years old!

> The work Digital has done on the ARM architecture has
> brought it up to levels where it can (yet again) compete on the Desktop's of
> the world.

It's not processors that compete for the desktop these days, it's
operating systems.  I think this pretty much keeps the ARM out of the
picture. (Unless Microsoft's grip is loosened by the OS neutral network
computers that are popping up, including Oracle's ARM based NC).  I
imagine tomorrow's announcement of a 500+ MHz PowerPC by Exponential
Technology will not vastly improve Apple's position in the marketplace. 
And DEC's Alpha running NT hasn't done well either, even though there is
a reasonable market for machines in it's price/performance range.

> Is there /really/ no market for very high power, very efficient, very
> inexpensive Multi-Processor systems? I just can't comprehend that...

There is no market for virtually any particular kind of computer
hardware, the market is for applications software.  I am unaware of a
mass market application that is popular enough to justify the creation
of an MP system for its execution.  Not that it won't happen someday,
but until then I'll be content with a 500MHz SA-1500 and it's
successors.  So if you want to see more progress, create a "killer app"
that fully exploits/requires MP.  Hardware sales will follow.  Sad to
say, the first application will probably be a game.  Video games are
already more powerful than desktop systems.

It does seem that the hardware folks are getting ahead of the software
folks doesn't it?

Cheers,
Scott



