Scott Kroeger <Scott.Kroeger@mei.com> writes:

>There is no market for virtually any particular kind of computer
>hardware, the market is for applications software.  I am unaware of a
>mass market application that is popular enough to justify the creation
>of an MP system for its execution.  Not that it won't happen someday,
>but until then I'll be content with a 500MHz SA-1500 and it's
>successors.  So if you want to see more progress, create a "killer app"
>that fully exploits/requires MP.  Hardware sales will follow.  Sad to
>say, the first application will probably be a game.  Video games are
>already more powerful than desktop systems.

It has also been stated that the main driving force of PC technology
is games: Games tend to be the first to exploit the full potential of
the newest hardware, which means that they become virtually unplayable
on maches more than a year old. Hence, PC home users will upgrade
(buy new systems) relatively often compared to e.g. Acorn users. And
when they do, they often buy the most powerful system that they can
lay their hands on. This drives the competition for providing ever
more powerful hardware. Microsoft (and other application software
providers) have then exploited the added power to add a lot of silly
and useless features to their systems.

It is however, not only the power of the machines that create software
bloat. It is also the size of the market. When there are millions of
users for a particular OS, application programs have very large
programming budgets. But adding more programmers do not always make a
product better, just larger.

Back to parallel machines. I doubt that personal computers will have
very much use of parallel hardware once yo have 200MHz or more
processing power, as most applications that would be able to exploit
parallelism (above 2-3 processors) tend to be i/o limited
(e.g. web-browsing). Few people have need of doing weather simulations
on their PC's. Also, the main cost of parallel systems is not the
processors, it is the interconnection. This reduces the advantage of
ARM processors.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)




