Sergio Riveros <riveros@capella.unm.edu> wrote:
>Hi I am interested the possibilitly of buying a Universal Desktop Box 
>(VX40A-F2) which I believe is the Multia.
>It has the CPU 166 Mhz DEC Alpha 21066A with 16K internal cache - 166 MHz i
>clock rate.
>
>I am planning on running Linux, the question, is how is the performance
>of the DEC VX40A-F2, compared to a pentium, are there any benchmarks for
>the pc that will measuare dhrystones accurately so that I can make a 
>comparison.
>I heard it has the performance of a pentium 75, is that correct ?

As a general rule, yes. Sometimes it really shines though; if you're
running cacheable programs, with not too many libm.a calls; it really
screams.
It's apparently pretty impressive with NFS for some reason.

>I am planning on running Linux Red Hat 4.0, any incompatibilities.?   

There are a number of problems with RH4.0, as it is the first AXP Linux
with shared libs.
In general, AXP-linux is buggier than x86 Linux, for obvious reasons.
>
>The other question, is that I have seen an ALPHA 233 on the digital page
>and it says the processor is upgradagle, does this apply to the VX40A-F2 multia.

Nope; only VX42 Multias have a ZIF slot. Of course, there is no upgrade
option for these anyway, as the 21066A doesn't have a faster successor
(though you can up the clock speed to 266Mhz from 233; you can't do
anything like this with the 166).

>
Not so much software as for x86 Linux.



