Torben AEgidius Mogensen wrote:
>
> It has also been stated that the main driving force of PC
> technology is games:
> ...
>
> Back to parallel machines. I doubt that personal computers
> will have very much use of parallel hardware once yo have
> 200MHz or more processing power, as most applications that
> would be able to exploit parallelism (above 2-3 processors)
> tend to be i/o limited (e.g. web-browsing).

Although combining the games and web browser idea...

My first Java applet was a game - a 3D Tetris game (cribbed off 
a PC game called 'Blockout') where you drop blocks into a cuboid 
well, and the blocks are animated as they spin, because it's 
completely impossible to play if they don't as you can't tell 
what's going on, due to the 3 degrees of freedom for spinning.  
I'll be putting this up on a web page shortly...

I'd be interested to know how well it runs on the StrongARM Java 
client that someone mentioned here recently, if anyone actually 
has access to it.  However, after much effort it's playable on 
Netscape 2 (non-JIT) on a 66MHz 486, and pretty darn smooth on a 
P133 with MSIE's JIT JVM, so I'd expect it to be rather good on 
a StrongARM, given that the client is claimed to be the fastest 
one out there.  (It doesn't use much FP...  Oh er, actually 
that's probably a lie, it uses FP throughout, but it's a fairly 
paltry amount compared to, say, ray tracing, or FEA, so it 
probably doesn't matter.)

Anyway, the interesting feature that is relevant here (honest) 
is that when I ran it on a dual-processor system, it actually 
made use of more than 1 processor.  (Most of the time, my PC's 
load sits at just over 50% when it's working hard, unless I 
deliberately give it 2 useful things to do.)  It was getting 
about 75% load.  In my attempts to make the game reasonably 
fast, I ended up with some moderately nifty multi-threaded 
animation code.  (Well it's smarter than Sun's example animator 
anyway, which breaks if you run it either on a slow machine, or 
with too high an animation rate...)  It's actually more or less 
impossible to do an animated game in Java without using 
multi-threading, so if Java games ever take off then 
multi-processor machines might actually be at an advantage.  But 
having a single processor running twice as fast is unfortunately 
better than two processors...


(The best thing I've found about dual processor PCs so far is 
that you can play games totally uninterrupted whilst running 
long installs or build in the background...  With 1 processor 
you either end up with the background process running incredibly 
slowly, or your game goes a bit jerkily.  An excellent use of 
technology IMO.  :-)  )


> Few people have need of doing weather simulations on their
> PC's. Also, the main cost of parallel systems is not the
> processors, it is the interconnection. This reduces the
> advantage of ARM processors.

Intel processors have had a fair amount of MP support since the 
Pentium.  2nd processor slots are fairly common.  More so on 
PPros.  (Well, all the ones I've seen have had an option for a 
second PPro, but that may just be the ones we happened to buy.)

So the cost of interconnection becomes, from the buyer's point 
of view at least, minimal.  This may lead to more widespread 
utilisation of MP.


But I'm sceptical myself.


--
Ian Griffiths



