In article <Ed.Wilts-ya023180001710961423350001@nntp.gov.bc.ca>,
    Ed.Wilts@gems1.gov.bc.ca (Ed Wilts) writes:

>>                         Customer testimonials as to how much faster things
>>                         are with Alpha.
> 
> We'd post if it were true, but frankly it isn't.  Price/performance, our
> good old Vaxes are the way to go.  The Alpha is so application dependent
> and memory intensive, and coupled with the high cost of re-licensing all
> the Vax software and migration applications, that the majority of users are
> staying Vax-based.  This is in an environment with 3,000 simultaneous VAX
> users and maybe 10 Alpha users.
> 

     I can certainly empathize with the cost of relicensing software
issue, and the application performance gain is certainly dependant
on the code, FP intensive stuff will show a huge gain, string oriented
programs will improve by much less than the VUPS/SPECMARKS might
suggest. A test of a month end for our financial application - lots of
strings and packed decimal - ran in about half the cpu time on an
Alphaserver 2100 4/200 (180 or so SpecInt) vs a MicroVax 3100/90
(24 VUP). Elapsed times were much closer together.

     However I'm not sure I follow you in terms of cost effectiveness. I
suppose this depends on a number of factors:

     - Comparing paid-off costs of VAX to cost of new Alpha
     - Cost of buying used VAX vs new Alpha
     - The class of system one is purchasing ( desktop, dept, enterprise )

    As a general statement I'd say Alphas have had substantial
price/performance improvements over the last 3 years, whereas VAX
systems haven't moved at all ( no new models - prices pretty much
the same )

    The Alphas are certainly more memory intensive, but then memory is
cheap ( recent price blip notwithstanding ) AS LONG AS YOU CAN GET
COMMODITY or 3RD PARTY MEMORY. Case in point - we have 4 alpha systems
here: An "Easy Web Server" (Multia), an Alphastation 250 4/266, an
AlphaServer 1000 4/266 and an Alphaserver 2100 4/200. All but the last
