In article <3266BFC6.3E54@aims.gov.au>, Kerry Jones <k.jones@aims.gov.au> writes:
> We need a program that will read VMS/RSTS format tapes on a Solaris box.
> Does anyone know of such a program??

  RSTS tapes come in three flavors: DOS, ANSI, and unlabeled. What's more
important is how the data got on the tape - recent RSTS/E versions (9.0 and
later) have a VMS-compatible BACKUP format. I don't know if the freebie
VMS BACKUP reader for Unix can handle these (it even has problems with some
VMS tapes), but VBACKUP from Boston Business Computing handles them, because
when BBC ported VBACKUP for me, I tested it with RSTS/E. BBC offers free
demos according to their web page (http://www.bbc.com).

  If the tapes aren't in BACKUP format, you'll need to provide more info as
to how they were written.

  Of course, if the files on the tape are things like indexed RMS files,
then you'll have more work getting them into a usable form. I seem to recall
a Canadian outfit, possibly called Software Translation, that had a RSTS
porting package for Unix that supported many system calls and also provided
an RMS interface.

