Mr. R. Tierney <kraisee@argonet.co.uk> writes:

>Check out :

>        http://www.ti/sc/docs/asic/0-18/018micro.htm
		^^^^^^
That should be www.ti.com

>Again, TI have made the point that the new 1.8 micron process can open up
>new technologies, they state that on one single 24mm2 chip they 'could'
>*/theoretically/* implement 30 Pentium Pro's! Alternatively 30 UltraSparcs
>is available!

>Then they cream the competition with the suggestion of 2,000 (yup, that's
>two /THOUSAND/   @-/   @-)   :-D )   ARM processors!!!

The TI page also says "It's about the combined total of all devices in
a high-end PC with 16 megabytes of RAM". I think this will be a more
interesting alternative: Build a 400MHz StrongARM together with all
the support chips (video etc.) plus the Piccolo DSP, FP and about 16MB
of RAM. With the RAM on the same chip as the processor, access time is
much reduced, so much that you can replace L1 cache by a small
(e.g. 2K) victim cache and use the DRAM buffers as L2 cache (see the
paper "breaking the memory wall" in the latest Computer Architecture
News). So essentially you get a very powerful RiscPC on a single chip.

The abovementioned article actually suggest using DRAM fabrication
technology and add a processor on a DRAM chip. The difference between
DRAM technology and processor technology is typically the number of
metal layers, which reduces the potential complexity of the
processors. The authors (being from Sun) suggest a simple SPARC
processor. However, ARM is much simpler than this, so it should be
ideal for such a construction.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)



