Mr. R. Tierney wrote:

> Firstly, the new 1.8 *0.18* micron process seems to be an excellent place for ARM &
> Digital to send their StrongARM designs to next. TI seem to be claiming that
> performance of 'greater than 500MHz' is now fairly strait-forward!!! They
> even list ARM Microprocessors as one possible device which could benefit.
> Assumedly, this does not exclude StrongARM, right?

Digital Semiconductor is (I think) the only licensee of the ARM
"architecture".  StrongARM was designed by Digital, not ARM.  I'm sure
Digital plans to migrate the StrongARM to their own 0.25 (and I presume
ever finer) geometry process.  The rest (including TI) are licensees of
particular ARM implementations.  So ARM does not own the StrongARM
design and it will be unlikely that TI will have access to it for their
TimeLine process.  However the ARM810 is available to TI.

> Blimey! That 1.8 *0.18* micron setup could even allow for Video controllers,
> IC-embedded VRAM (lots of it!), IO controllers etc to be integrated in one
> single very-high powered chip - /and/ it would still be very cheap (compared
> to rivals anyway).

Digital Semiconductor has already announced their plan for a highly
integrated StrongARM SA-1500, which should have everything mentioned
except memory.

> This /could/ be exciting, if anybody finds they really must do something
> with this... (I pray).

I'd be much more excited by Digital and Xilinx merging the SA-1500 with
10K+ gates of Xilinx 6200 series FPGA (or TI and Xilinx merging the
ARM7500 or ARM810 with same).  Wouldn't that make a nice little
package!?  I'd know what to do with that combination.  I'm certain I'd
have no use for 2000 StrongARMs on a single die.  Even minor parallelism
is overkill for many embedded applications (ARM will not take over the
desktop market) and the state of the art in massively parallel computers
won't produce many (if any) mass market applications for that 2000
StrongARM die.

I'd also like to see large amounts of DRAM on the die, primarily for the
speed increase and reduction of EMI from minimizing external bus
activity.

I also don't think TI's TimeLine process is anything revolutionary.  The
rest of the world is right behind them.  So we can expect more for less
from everybody!

Regards,
Scott

