Hello Stephen,

> There are around 20 ARM licensees I know of.

Then you know of 5 more than ARM, at last count they list 15 on their
web page.

> I would like to know where you get your information from.

I get my info from:
  The local Digital Semiconductor field application engineer (who
explained their architecture license)
  The local Digital Semiconductor technical rep
  The distributor through which we purchase our StrongARM processors
  Digital's web page
  ARM's web page
  Various magazine articles

> For example, StrongARM is a trademark of
> ARM LTd, not Digital Semiconductor.

Yes, and I did not claim otherwise.

The other licensees do not have the right to alter the architecture of
the processor.  In effect they get their designs from ARM and implement
them in their process and wrap their own enhancements AROUND the
processor core.  The SA-110 is Digital's own implementation of the ARM
architecture (certainly with help from ARM).  So while you may see
ARM610's, 710's, 810's etc from other vendors, you will not see anyone
else make the SA-110 unless Digital lets it happen.  I don't see any
reason for Digital to do that, so I expect them to be the sole provider
of the SA family for now.

Of course there is nothing (but time and money) to prevent TI from
pushing the ARM810 into the StrongARM's domain using their TimeLine
process, but remember, Digital has a nice process too!

> We could use an FPGA + SA combination as well.

I think this is a killer idea!

Cheers,
Scott



