In article <DzCC4z.AFF@world.std.com> craigp@world.std.com 
(Craig Partridge) writes:
>    I was reading through the 21164 hardware and discovered a set of
>instructions with a /V in the name (ADDL/V, ADDQ/V, MULQ/V, etc).

>What does the /V stand for (it isn't anywhere in my Alpha Architecture
>Reference Manual) and what does it cause the instructions to do differently
>(other than load a different op code :-)).

	My Alpha Architecture Reference Manual (paperback, 1992)
lists ADDL on p. 4-23, section 4.4.1.  Under Qualifiers, it lists

               Integer Overflow Enable (/V)

I haven't tried ADDL/V, but I suspect it causes an integer overflow
exception if the sum of two positive values exceeds 2^31-1,
or the sum of two negative values is less than -2^31.
Some languages, such as Ada and LISP, require overflow detection.
I like unsigned arithmetic (modulo 2^32) and don't want an add to abort.
