Han Brinkman (hanbr@worldaccess.nl) wrote:
: I like to know how Baan software is behaving on a Dec Alpha cluster
: running under Digital Unix. What happens to the Unix processes when
: one systems doesn't work anymore. Are the other systems in the
: cluster taking over the processes running on the failing system?
: Does that mean that memory/process info are shared between several
: system?
: Can anyone give me info where to find more info how a cluster behaves?

I've never heard of Bean software, so it is hard to decide how
to answer your question.  If the product runs on top of a database
such as Oracle Parallel Server, you can take great advantage of
Memory Channel and should therefore look into the TruCluster
Production Server Software.  A technical article describing this
is in this month's Digital Technical Journal, which is online at:

    http://www.digital.com/info/DTJ/

If the product is not very database-oriented you might want to look
into the TruCluster Available Server Software.  A technical article
describing this is in a back issue of DTJ at:

    http://www.digital.com:80/.i/info/DTJK00/

There is a top-level page at:

    http://www.unix.digital.com/cluster/index.html

To summarize the operation, the cluster software montitors the state of
cluster members.  When one member departs the cluster, another member
that has been predefined as being able to run the service is told to
run the service.  The cluster software does this by invoking a shell
script with arguments describing the service that should be started.
If the service is stateful and the old member terminated ungracefully, 
the new service does any necessary cleanup before offering the service.
It can readily do so because all services have access to all storage
devices.

This is probably an oversimplfication.  I'm sure the web resources
will do a better job at describing the products.

