In article <541fjv$djo@news-e2b.gnn.com>, jbringolf@uncfsu.campus.mci.net writes:
>
>Have an HSZ between two 7000's and have 4 raid sets in use with 2.1G drives. 
>Want to replace all sets with 4.2G drives. System has a mixture of UFS and 
>ADVFS partitions. Has anyone accomplished a swap like this before. If so any 
>hints, tips or potholes you encountered would be appreciated. If someone has 
>information but needs additional information please email and I'll add the 
>details.

If you have the slots for one group of new disks and the existing the
group, then the swap should be pretty easy.  For the AdvFS file system
with the AdvFS tools it gets even easier.

1.  Insert new drives and ADD them.
2.  Create and initialize the RAID.
3.  ADD the Unit.

You can scu(8) to rescan the bus to find the new logical unit; no
rebooting necessary:

# scu scan edt bus _
# scu show edt bus _

fill in the appropriate bus number.  Then create the special device
files for the new logical unit.  If you have the AdvFS utilities
you add the block device of the new RAID to the domain and then
remove the old volume.  When the command is complete the data will
have been migrated to the new volume.

For UFS you'll have to backup the data and restore it to the
new device after creating a file system on it.  You'll want
to unmount the file system and use dump/restore.  You can
use dump/restore across a pipe (patch or V3.2C dump needed
on V4.0).

