Trip Martin  (tm@squonk.net) wrote:

> I took a look inside my multia last night (233MHz, if it makes any
> difference), and there was what looked like a 72 pin SIMM, and two
> 30pin SIMMs.  There was only 1 slot for a 72 pin SIMMs and four
> slots four 30 pin SIMMs.  The machine is supposed to have 24MB,
> so I'm assuming the 72pin SIMM is 16MB and the others are 4MB.
> It still seems like a very odd setup.
> 

	The memory in all of the multias is 72 pin ECC memory.  The
SRAM is on a 168 pin card on the versions of the mother board that
have a single socket.  Early versions had either soldered on SRAM or
11 sockets for chips.  In general, the 166MHz boards came with
soldered cache and CPU, or 166MHz socketed CPU and soldered SRAM or
233MHZ with socketed SRAM and a socketed CPU.  The CPU socket was for
upgrading the speed.  Although the board was designed for 266 and
300MHz versions of the CPU, they were never released to my knowledge.
I know of people who have over clocked the CPU successfully, though
the higher speeds require cool rooms and additional fans.  Bear in
mind the power supply on the basic box does not have much extra power.

	I wonder if you might have counted about half the pins?

	DEC did put an unusual 12MB memory board in some of the
machines, but it has 72 pins.

	All the best,

	Peter David



