I can appreciate your frustration.   The setup instructions for this
device leave a lot to be desired.  But I can help...

First of all,  let me tell you that *32-bit* soundcard drivers for
this card have FINALLY been released by Diamond.   I've installed them
& found them to work quite nicely.   Upon re-booting after setup,
there is even a prompt to setup/enable the DOS-mode functionality.
They can only be  obtained by downloading them from the Digital PC
Software Library at:

	http://www.pc.digital.com/~ftp/index/ix_Starion_9xx.htm

The filename is AD90102B.EXE.  Get the corresponding text file,
AD90102B.TXT,  as well.  I strongly recommend reading this & the
diskette's README.TXT thoroughly before installing.

Otherwise...

...If you want to use the utilities shipped with the system,
you MUST have a copy of the "Digital Starion Restore CD"
which contains all the setup files for the pre-loaded software &
drivers.   There you will find a directory [L:\RESTORE\FAXTALK\DISK1]
Note: If you do not have this directory, you may have an older version
of the restore CD & should get an updated one by requesting it from
Starion support (800-554-3333, opt 1)

From within Win95 in the above directory of the restore CD, launch the
setup program, TCSETUP.EXE which installs the soundcard drivers
& continues on to install the bundled Faxtalk program which can be
aborted as it doesn't effect the soundcard setup in any way.
(FYI: Don't look for the Telecommander card to appear under
Device-Manager. With the original 16-bit drivers, it doesn't.)

For DOS-level support (ie: DOS games, etc) you will need to download
an appropriate configuration file (check which CDROM drive you have)
from the same WWW address as above to enable DOS-mode sound,
mouse, & CDROM support.

This should always work unless you have another device competing for
the default system resources that the Telecommander is configured for.

I hope this information is useful to you.  Good luck.

Travis Crow
PC Offsite Services
Digital Equipment Corp.



