                  DIGITAL'S PALMER AND MICROSOFT'S GATES
                 TO KICK-OFF SYMPOSIUM AT DECUS '96/ANAHEIM

        November 9-14 Marks the Dates for an Information Explosion
            for IT Professionals Using Digital-Based Technology


LITTLETON, MA - OCTOBER 15, 1996 - Digital Equipment Corporation Chairman 
Robert Palmer and Microsoft Chairman Bill Gates are the headliners for the 
DECUS '96/Anaheim Seminars, Symposium, and Trade Show, running November 
9-14 at the Anaheim Convention Center. As the opening keynote speakers on 
November 11, Palmer and Gates will address the strategic directions of 
Digital and Microsoft, and the importance of their alliance.

DECUS '96/Anaheim will have a vast array of educational, informative and 
demonstrative activities taking place for the six-day event. On the 
educational side there are intensive full-day technical seminars November, 
9-10 and shorter one- to two-hour symposium sessions including introductory 
to advanced sessions and concept overviews, November 11-14. On the 
demonstrative side, November 11-13, attendees can go to the 75-plus vendor 
tradeshow that showcases hands-on demonstrations of new and recently 
released products and services. On the interactive side, the DECUS 
Campgrounds offer attendees the chance to run demos on the hardware and 
software they hear about in the seminars and symposium sessions.

The DECUS event is geared toward six major focal areas: the Internet; 
Information and Database Management; Network Design and Management; System 
Management in a Client/Server Environment; Integration/Migration; and 
Application Development. Sessions are presented in a real-world context 
with offerings that address scenarios under the Windows NT, Digital UNIX, 
and OpenVMS operating systems.

The all-day intensive training seminars held on November 9 and 10 include 
such topics as: Internet Server and Application Sizing, OpenVMS for System 
Managers V5.5 Through V7.0, Integration and Management of the TCP/IP 
Desktop Environment, and Storage Technology, I/O Performance and RAID. 
During the symposium, November 11-14, each day offers a myriad of more than 
100 one- to two-hour sessions -- from introductory level to comprehensive 
technical overviews -- allowing attendees to both learn new technologies, 
and to sharpen their skills.

In addition to the Palmer/Gates keynote address on November 11, two other 
keynotes are set:
* Strategies for the Internet and Intranet - on November 12 featuring Ilene 
  Lang, vice president of Digital's Software Business Unit, and John 
  Sviokla, associate professor, Harvard Business School.
* Imagine the Future - on November 13, featuring William Strecker Digital's 
  chief technology officer and senior corporate consulting engineer, and 
  Gordon Bell, senior researcher with Microsoft's Telepresence Research 
  Group. A panel of six Digital senior consulting engineers will follow 
  with their views on where the industry and technology are headed and how 
  it will affect information technology professionals.

For more information, or to register to attend, contact DECUS Customer 
Service at 1-800-DECUS55. Outside the U.S. and Canada, call 508-952-3410. 
Complete program information is also available on the DECUS U.S. Chapter 
Internet Web server at URL http://www.decus.org.

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS celebrates its 35th anniversary this year. DECUS, 
the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is an association of 
information technology professionals interested in the products, services, 
and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and related vendors. The 
Society's purpose is to promote the unimpeded exchange of information, with 
the goal of helping its members and their organizations to be more 
successful. The U.S. Chapter provides each member with means to enhance 
their professional development, forums for technical training, mechanisms 
for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy programs and opportunities 
for informal disclosure and interaction with professional colleagues of 
like interests.

