>
>In article <325C76EE.5F7EC039@mit.edu>, "Michael P. Phillips"
><mpp@mit.edu> wrote:
>
>> Hello,
>>    I am the proud new owner of a MicroVax II. Now, I'd really like to
>> hook up a terminal up to it as the console. I poked around the web alot
>> but could not locate info or pinouts on what I'd liek to do...
>> There's this 9-pin male D-shell connector which I believe is for the
>> console. I've seen references to a device which splits this into 3 MMJ
>> outputs, one of which can be connected to a VT220 terminal through
>> another connector I think. All I want to do is hack together a cable
>> to plug into the 9-pin on one end and my VT220 on the other. Any ideas?
>
>I and a friend also just got MicroVAX II's and we had the same problem. 
>We were able to hook up an IBM's COM port to the VAX's console port using
>a 9-pin to 25-pin straight-thru, connected to a 25-pin to 25-pin straight
>thru to a 25-pin to 9-pin adapter which went on the back of the IBM.  I
>suppose you could have run a 9-pin to 9-pin straight thru cable, but we
>either didn't find one, or didn't bother to look (I can't remember...at
>least it works).
>

The Microvax II's 9-pin serial port is wired differently than a
PC's 9-pin serial port. DEC designed it a couple of years  before
IBM redesigned a different format for the PC.

The pins are (I may have this NULL from what it should be)

	1 - Protective ground
	2 - Transmit data
	3 - Receive data
	4 - RTS
	5 - DTR
	6 - DSR
	7 - Signal Ground

So, to make a simple 9-to-25 cable, connect
	2 - 2, 3 - 3, 7 - 7
or, if I have this NULL
	2 - 3, 3 - 2, 7 - 7
(three wires is all it takes for a terminal)




