In article <53481l$tug@onlink3.onlink.net>, kwparker@onlink.net wrote:
>HELP!
>
>
>
>Any pointers?  The system doesn't have NETCONFIG.COM on it, for some reason. 
> I've tried messing around with NCP to define circuits and nodes, but I'm not 
>getting anywhere.  The system can't even recognize the circuit (SVA-0) that 
>it connects to the network with.  I'm wondering if there is some other 
>physical damage to the machine (circuit, etc.).  The replacement hard drive 
>is configured to the same state that it was in prior to the DEC consultant 
>connecting it to the network.  He used LAT at one point, that I do know.
>Your help is greatly appreciated.
>
>Kim
>kwparker@onlink.net
>

The first question is there a license on the system for DECNET?
Try typing a SHOW LICENSE command at your DCL prompt.
You should see a license like either DVNETRTG or DVNETEND. If not, you can't 
get it running, the circuit well not start with the NCP command
NCP>SET CIRC SVA-0 STATE ON
You will get an error returned if the license is not there
You could try LAT though, to test connectivity. If the other system has DEcnet 
and Lat running e.g. has Decservers on it, try this.
@SYS$STARTUP:LAT$STARTUP
MC LATCP
latcp>SET NODE/CONNECTIONS=BOTH
latcp>SHO SERVICES
latcp>EXIT
This allows you outbound connections to the services listed, one should be 
your other computer., if you see no services, then somethings broken!
You can now connect to the other computer by typing.
SET HOST/LAT XXXXXX  (the systems service name)
You should get your USERNAME: prompt from the system.
If you got this far, the hardware is fine..The net$config.com file should be 
in the sysmanager account, unless someone installed decnet OSI, then ncp can't 
help you any way. You need to use NCL..Thats a whole new ball game..We'll 
leave that till you need to know about this, if you are using OSI.

Good Luck!!



