In article <5348ps$44i@dfw-ixnews2.ix.netcom.com>, j
hre@ix.netcom.com(J.A.Harvey) writes:
|>It doesn't look like I'm in the right newsgroup ( every post seems to
|>deal with the larger DEC machines). But I'll give it a try and hope
|>that someone will take pity on me!
|>
|>I have a Starion 915 ( 100mhz Pentium ) upgraded to 40 meg ram. The
|>problem is that DEC put a Telecommander sound/modem/popcorn maker/oil
|>filter card in this machine ( 14400 Baud ). Does anyone know how I can
|>upgrade this to a faster ( 33,600 ) modem without going through the
|>hoops that I have been through lately? I remove the Telecomander card
|>and replace it with the newer modem and all hell breaks loose. System
|>freeze, no boot the whole ball of wax.

Did you add in a separate sound card as well?  I don't think the popcorn
maker is upgradeable (but the cupholder is...)

Try this...  Power on, and press F2 to get into the BIOS setup.  Select the
Advanced page and change "Reset configuration data?" to "Yes".  Save and exit.
This will clear the "Plug and Play" info that the system has stored, allowing
it to be recomputed.  Also make sure you have no IRQ conflicts with the new
board(s).

I think that there is a jumper on the Telecommander board to disable just
the modem, or you could install the new modem on a different COM port
(avoid COM4 - I have heard that this can sometimes cause conflicts with
S3 video adapters.)
