In article <86afu4ro35.fsf@barsoom.Hamartun.Priv.NO>, Tom I Helbekkmo <tih@hamartun.priv.no> writes:
:I need a bit of help diagnosing a problem with a TK50 SCSI tape
:station (the one with the TK50 and SCSI hardware in a shoebox with a
:handle on the back).  It often works fine, but has two failure modes
:that show up together or separately, off and on.

    The particular model of TK50-series SCSI tape drive would be
    quite useful, as there are several variants of the "TK50" drive
    available, all of which meet the physical description provided.
    (Some of these drives were sold with the VAXstation and MicroVAX
    2000 series, and these drives are not compatible with SCSI.  A
    ROM upgrade is/was available to convert these drives into SCSI
    drives.)  The particular host and the host controller would also
    be of interest, as these drives are not compatable with certain
    types of hosts and SCSI interfaces.

:The first symptom is that the tape station will be sitting with a
:green light (either empty or with a loaded tape), and then all of a
:sudden make that snapping noise that it makes when you've inserted a
:tape and tell it to load, wait a bit, and then (with a softer snap) go
:back to green light idle.  This will usually happen several times,
:with varying intervals.

   This sounds like a problem with the "hammerhead" -- this is the
   "hook" that mates with the loop on the end of the tape.  If there
   is a problem with the "hammerhead", this usually means either the
   hammerhead (or the drive) needs to be swapped out.

:The other failure mode is a situation where the tape station goes into
:a catatonic state, with the green light flickering unsteadily with no
:discernible pattern, then going out for a second or three, then
:flickering again, and so forth.  During this time the tape station
:will not respond to SCSI commands.  It can enter this state from the
:middle of a tape operation.

   It is unclear what is going on here.  This could be a problem
   with the drive, with the SCSI, with the host controller, etc.

