In article <3255C71D.639C@uncfsu.campus.mci.net>, John Bringolf <jbringolf@uncfsu.campus.mci.net> writes:
:Is it possible to delete the disk quote files in the root level 
:of each device. I do not have the quota options enabled but have
:files growing to over 17M in some directories.

  You don't indicate and you've posted to the "generic" group, so
  assuming this is OpenVMS...

  $ SET PROCESS/PRIVI=ALL
  SYSMAN> DISKQUOTA DISABLE/DEVICE=ddcu:
  $ DELETE ddcu:[000000]QUOTA.SYS;

  Alternatively, leave the quotas enabled and set the quota limit on each
  user and on the [0,0] default user to a value larger than the capacity
  of the disk -- this allows you to continue to use the diskquota tool to
  monitor the total disk usage on a per-user basis, but without enforcing
  any particular per-user diskquotas.

