> >> [The PDP-8 emulator on the 11/60] probably was used a
> >> bit for the DECmate, as that series ran the WPS software on the 8
> >> micro.  I don't know if we ever offered it to the outside world.
> >
> >That seems a bit unclear to me.  How would [the emulator] be helpful 
> >for the DECmate, which was based on [a PDP-8 on a chip]?
> 
> It didn't.  The 6120 was the target processor, but development was
> (apparently) done on an 11/60 with microcode to emulate the PDP-8
> architecture.  I believe all this was done under RSTS; with loadable
> run-time systems, a "PDP-8" RTS could be run which kick in the "PDP-8"
> microcode on the 11/60 and emulate the I/O.
> 
> I'm pretty sure that the PDP-8 microcode for the 11/60 never escaped 
> from DEC, and was loaded into the (optional) WCS on the /60.

All correct.  The WPS development group had a pair of RSTS 11/60's that
it used for PDP-8 emulation.  Besides providing a much more congenial
development environment than WPS-8, they were the fastest "PDP-8's" ever
built.

As I understand it, the PDP-8 emulation RTS for RSTS would actually run
on *any* RSTS system.  It included a complete PDP-8 emulation in PDP-11
code, sufficiently complete to boot OS/8 (under which WPS code was
developed, if I remember right).  Used that way, it wasn't necessarily
very fast - but it worked.  When running on an 11/60, the RTS would load
the WCS and let the microcode do the emulation.

The RTS design of RSTS was really a very nice feature, which I miss to
this day.  (I did a patched version of BASIC PLUS called "OWNKBM". 
Whenever it gained control, instead of starting you off in an empty
session, it executed a program called KBM.  I provided a "shell" KBM.BAS
which provided things like a variety of built-in commands, then a
fallback to commands RSTS knew about, and finally a search for a file
with the same name as the command.  You could extend it easily by
changing some tables.  Quite handy, really.)

Going back to the 11/60:  I once saw a demo of an extended version of
the PDP-8 emulator, probably run in conjunction with a GT-40.  It put up
a picture of a PDP-8's front panel - with active displays and switches!
I don't know if even the WPS group ever received that hack.

As far as I know, DEC never provided any form of the -8 emulator to
outside customers.  (I knew about it only because I did some work with
the WPS group.)
							-- Jerry



