In article <moxness-0410961804380001@news.ucalgary.ca>, moxness@acs.ucalgary.ca (Jack Moxness) writes:
|Hello VMS/OpenVMS experts (I am a Macintosh specific guru myself),
|
|   I need to help a laboratory in our dept. get a DECNet functioning between
|an aging MicroVAX II (with VMS 4.4) and a newer AlphaStation 200 with OpenVMS
|6.1 Apparently this did function at some time in the past - but no more.
|I believe the Alpha also has pathworks software to allow Macintoshes in the
|lab to 'speak' to it, but the priority is to move files between the MicroVAX
|and Alpha.
|
|   There is a coax ethernet cable running between the 2 systems.
|   I have set the Alpha's ewa_0 ON (should use the coax not the twisted-pair?)
|
|What are some easy to follow step by step instructions for configuring the
|software. There are multiple manuals which refer to networking. They are
|complicated by the need to describe running over ASYNC or SYNC lines etc.
|I only want something simple. (On a Mac I just go into a simple Network
|control panel and click on a different "type"). This Alpha/VAX stuff is all
|command line based and I get lost in trying to follow the steps.
|
|This should really be simple just to get 2 machines sending files back and
|forth over this coax ethernet - shouldn't it? How do I get started?

First, let's assume that you've got these things cabled correctly,
and also that you have DECnet licensed for these boxes.

So you'll need to make up some node names (6 chars max) - let's say
VAX and ALPHA - and some DECnet addresses - let's say 1.1 and 1.2.

Then on each node just do this:

$ @SYS$MANAGER:NETCONFIG

follow the prompts.  Neigher of these nodes needs to be a routing
node as they're both on the same Ethernet cable.

Start up DECnet (@SYS$MANAGER:STARTNET - which I think can be 
automatically done as an option from NETCONFIG) and you should
have your DECnet running.

You should then be able to copy files back and forth with
COPY - e.g.

$ COPY 1"username password"::dev:[dir]filename.ext whatever

You can make life a little easier by defining DECnet proxy 
records so you don't have to type the username/password stuff.
You can also define the hostnames in each others databases -
e.g.

$ MCR NCP 
NCP> SET NODE ALPHA ADDRESS 1.2
NCP> DEF NODE ALPHA ADDRESS 1.2
etc.

If you run into problems, or would like to know more about
what's going on, you'd better check out the VMS doc set.
_DECnet for OpenVMS Guide to Networking_ is probably a good
place to start.

Yeah, DECnet is harder to set up than AppleTalk, but it's a
heckuva lot easer than TCP/IP!

Aaron

