Don't forget the cheap, low-tech solution that does not require buying any
equipment, etc: Kermit.  It's available for MVS too.

Aside from cost, weight, machine-room footprint, etc, Kermit has another
distinct advantage: it can translate character sets between VMS (say DEC MCS,
DEC Kanji, various NRCs, etc) to the corresponding EBCDIC-based IBM Country
Extended Code Pages (CECPs) -- important if you are transferring textual
information written in almost any language other than English (or Latin :-).

Even when you *have* a TCP/IP connection between VMS and MVS, you can use
Kermit to transfer files over it when you need this feature, or any of its
other features, such as automated, unattended operation, etc.

More about Kermit at:

  http://www.columbia.edu/kermit/

- Frank



