In article <52os42$n3m@ns1.ccinet.net>, fraggle@tsrcom.com (Drumroll Please) writes:
:I currently am having problems printing files in which i have an <ESC>
:sequence in it, a gold 27.  The problem is not with the printing of
:the file, but with printing the <ESC> out on the printer.  For some
:reason it ignores it altogether in the output.
:
:Can someone please tell me how I can get the printer to print out the
:<ESC> on the hardcopy?

  <GOLD>27 is -- in all likelyhood -- a key sequence used in a text
  editor that accepts a specified keycode and inserts the associated
  ASCII character.

  An escape character, by itself, isn't necessary valid nor printable.

  The behaviour of a device that receives an escape character is
  dependent on the particular output device -- the terminal or
  printer(s) involved.  Many printers and video terminals expect that
  characters such as the <ESC> and <CSI> characters will `introduce'
  a sequence of characters, often called an "escape sequence".

  Escape sequences can be used to control fonts, locate the cursor,
  set margins, etc -- there are a number of standard sequences, and
  a number of device- or vendor-specific escape sequences... 

