I used to recommend that people get a Unibus SCSI controller and some
modern small SCSI disks.  Since the rapid plummet in RA7n and RA9n
prices, though, it's become much more attractive to hook these up
to a UDA50 (UDA50's have been selling for $50 a set in North America
for years now...)  You get the advantages of cheap high capacity disks
along with a *fully* supported controller.

>  (I have a 
>small tower box full of ESDI disks, which cost me the grand total of an 
>hour's use of my 9track tape drive...)

ESDI disks are also a good way to go.  They tended to be built much
more durably than the MFM disks of the same era.  

I use ESDI disks on Q-bus systems with
Webster controllers.  Faster than most SCSI controllers (the 1 Mbyte
of cache on the controller doesn't hurt!), and the
ability to partition in the controller firmware is really nice for
multiple OS's on the same physical disk.  For example, I have a 1.0
Gbyte Hitach ESDI with 2.11BSD, RSX-11M+ v4.2, and three different
versions of RT-11.  And there are still three scratch partitions which
get used as necessary.  Did Webster ever make any ESDI controllers
for the Unibus?

Tim.



