carlini@krakar.enet.dec.com (Antonio Carlini) writes:

>The 68000-based DSV11 Q-bus synch controller has downloadable microcode. The
>other (non-Q-bus) synch controllers that use downloadable microcode are the
>DSW series, the DSB32 and the DSF32.

>I believe that the KMS/KMV series can also have downloadable code (at least
>there appear to be customer tools to allow you to develop such code) but I
>don't know much about these beasts.
KMS was really a productized version of KMC11.
DMC11 is same instruction set, rom(PROM) based processor, 330 ns cycle.
KMC11 is the RAM based unit with 180ns cycle (and an Inst Reg added
to DMC design to allow faster execution while next instruction is fetched).
KMV (sorry can't recall the designers name) was the Qbus version based
on 6502 cpu if I recall correctly (got 5 of them upstairs and am too
lazy to go look).

As a data point:
the KMC/DMC and 74s181 based derivatives are harvard architecture
processors.

THe 11/60 (Jim OLoughlin was the lead) is really the same processor
implemented by a different team, and aimed at PDP11 emulation.

Richie Lary was called in at the end of both sets of 
projects to bum the microcode.  The legend is that both 
processors have a microcoded emulation of a pdp8 embedded in them
'cause that was/is (?) Richie's trademark.

just my two cents ....
bob

