Patrick Gainer wrote:

> The stated purpose of the benchmarks is ostensibly to provde
> somewhat realistic workloads, or at least to provide maximum
> configuration benchmarks. While the TPC is made from vendors,
> the benchmarks are clearly intended for customer comparisions
> so how could anyone object to a Full Disclosure about the disk
> subsystem?
> 
> I guess I'll have to begin championing two modifications to the
> general TPC benchmarks now:
> 
>         1, Full disclosures MUST be done only with hardware and
>            software which is currently generally availaable.
> 
>         2, Full disclosures MUST include the total count of all
>            components used in the benchmark, INCLUDING all failed
>            components.

In my personal experience, the biggest problem in "reproducing" one
of these high-performance database configurations isn't the hardware,
or even the software availability, as it is the availability of the
*combination* of software needed.  Sure, the hardware is available,
sooner or later, sure, the database is available, sooner or later,
as is the OS.  But, just try getting the specified versions of the
OS and *all of* database software to work, along with the inevitable 
required performance and bugfix patches  [sometimes these patches are 
special database "features"], to work reliably in toto.  So,
"availability"
isn't fine-grained enough.  If something like this is worth championing,
it must be the case that the *required combination* is all working
together
in the absence of the system vendor's OS and database gurus and the
database vendor's tiger team.  That is, that the specified shipping
OS/patches/features and database will work together at the customer's
site.

(Then again, maybe I'm the only person in the world who has had
diffculty
with getting all the right stuff to work together as advertised.)



