to: James Bonfield <jkb@mrc-lmb.cam.ac.uk>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: CTG	
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0Gold (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)


James Bonfield wrote:
> 
> So, the big question is, what's the official stance of which binaries
are
> compatible with which OS releases? How does dynamic (probably best) versus
> static linking change this?

The 'official stance' is that we guarantee upwards compatibility of user
mode
applications.   I.E. if you built it on an older OS release, it should
run on
*any* newer OS release.  If it doesn't, it's a bug and we will fix it.
 
> Some specific examples are: we currently compile under OSF/1 V3.0. We really
> ought to upgrade to 3.2C at the least, if not 4.0. However can 4.0 binaries be
> ran under 3.0 and 3.2? 

Officially, you can't move things backwards.   Sometimes it works if you 
link -non_shared, but it isn't guaranteed to, and there are definitely
cases
where it doesn't work.  (For example, in V4.0, the pathconf() function
changed
from a library routine to a syscall.   Old kernels don't have that
syscall,
so programs that use pathconf() and compiled on V4.0 won't work on
V3.x).

>Can 3.0 binaries be run under 4.0? (Past record show
> this to be likely - I'm even still running OSF/1 BL9 and ultrix converted
> binaries now.)

See above.

