        Last week, I asked a  couple  of questions about using SCSI tape
    drives on HSC's.  Briefly, I said:

In article <1996Sep30.174900.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>, 
    			fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu writes:

>     Rumor #1	"Don't put SCSI tapes on an HSC with disks." 
>     --------
>         Well, I know  you  don't  put  tapes  on  a requestor with disks
>     (can't  mix'n'match), but different requestors on the same HSC?   Is
>     this really a problem?  ...

[...]
>     Rumor #2	"Put each SCSI tape on its own SCSI bus."
>     --------
>         OK, this one I _might_  buy.   I  can  imagine that trying to do
>     backups  to two tape drives which are on the same SCSI bus (and same
>     k.scsi requestor) could lead to contention for the bus and less than
>     optimal performance.  But are separate  busses  a  requirement? ...

        I guess there aren't many of us left managing large VMSclusters,
    or at least, do  that  _and_  reading  usenet.   :-)  I got just one
    e-mail  reply,  from Ed Wilts <Ed.Wilts@gems1.gov.bc.ca> (thanks  Ed
    :-).  He confirmed that there's no problem running  SCSI  tapes  and
    disks  on  the same HSC (or HSJ), and that he'd successfully run two
    TZ8x7 tapes (with loaders) on a  single  SCSI bus on an HSJ40.  I'll
    assume I can do the same on an HSC95...pending very poor performance
    to  the  contrary...and I'm querying DSNlink to see what they  might
    have to say as well.

        I'm still a bit  surprised  there  aren't  more of you out there
    with some experience in this area...

        -Ken
