It always seemed to me that UNIBUS SCSI controllers were (a) about the
same vintage/technology as their Qbus brethren, and (b) of less appear, 
and therefore more expensive.

>                          Since the rapid plummet in RA7n and RA9n
>prices, though, it's become much more attractive to hook these up
>to a UDA50 (UDA50's have been selling for $50 a set in North America
>for years now...)  You get the advantages of cheap high capacity disks
>along with a *fully* supported controller.

Thes, the RA9n drives are good, athough they're still pretty big (they 
mount in a heavy aluminium extrusion) and still pretty noisy.  They don't 
bite the dust in two years like an RA8n though.

I'm yet to see an RA7n drive.  Did anyone buy any?  I got the distinct 
impression that most of that generation of drives sold were SCSI and DSSI 
variants (ie RZ7n and RF7n) rather than the DSA disks.  

>I use ESDI disks on Q-bus systems with
>Webster controllers.  Faster than most SCSI controllers (the 1 Mbyte
>of cache on the controller doesn't hurt!), and the

I have Webster controllers too.  They go like a scalded cat.  When I
first got a Webster, I put it in with a ~150MB ESDI disk (I think it was
a Fujitsu drive -- it went farming a year or two ago), about a 30ms
access time.  It replaced an RD53, which is a 36ms drive, although about
half the capacity.  The ESDI/Webster combo just left the old RD53/RQDX3
setup standing; things that took a minute on the RD53 took under ten
seconds on the ESDI drive.  I was very, very happy.  8-)

The only caveat is that I have anecdotal evidence that media errors can
go undetected.  A site a friend of mine works at had problems with a disk
that was on its way out just losing data without actually logging errors.
I don't know enough about the low levels of ESDI or the exact nature of
the fault to say whether there is a possibility of this happening without
the controller knowing; either way replacing the disk (I think just the 
HDA in the end) cleared up the problem (and hastened the delivery of some 
of the drives and controllers into my sticky little fingers 8-).

>                        Did Webster ever make any ESDI controllers
>for the Unibus?

Not that I'm aware of.

