Eric Smith <eric@goonsquad.spies.com> wrote:
>In article <536fa8$4ob@atlas.xylogics.com> mitton@xylogics.com (Dave Mitton) writes:
>> 	That's not a rumor.  I missed the original post, but the 11/60 did
>> indeed have user modifiable microcode.  I took the class once.  The
>> PDP-8 emulator package was called "8.5 x 11".   It probably was used a
>> bit for the DECmate, as that series ran the WPS software on the 8
>> micro.  I don't know if we ever offered it to the outside world.
>
>That seems a bit unclear to me.  How would "8.5 x 11", which was microcode
>for the PDP-11/60, be helpful for the DECmate, which was based on the
>Intersil/Harris 6120 (a PDP-8 on a chip)?

It didn't.  The 6120 was the target processor, but development was
(apparently) done on an 11/60 with microcode to emulate the PDP-8
architecture.  I believe all this was done under RSTS; with loadable
run-time systems, a "PDP-8" RTS could be run which kick in the "PDP-8"
microcode on the 11/60 and emulate the I/O.

I'm pretty sure that the PDP-8 microcode for the 11/60 never escaped from 
DEC, and was loaded into the (optional) WCS on the /60.

