        I have a couple of questions  about tape drives, SCSI, and HSC's
    motivated by some "rumors" that have been floated around my site.

        Just to  prime  your  sympathy  juices,  :-)  I'll  say  that we
    currently  do  backups  to a pair of Exabyte 8200 drives on  an  MTI
    controller hosted by an old HSC50 on our CI.  (Did I hear  you  say,
    "Ouch!"  or  "Ohmygawd!"?)  The  HSC50 has _only_ these tapes on it,
    nothing else.

        OTHO, we're about to take possession of some new equipment and I
    plan to retire the Exabytes and  the  HSC50, and replace them with a
    TZ87 and a TZ88 (the TZ87's been here, unused, for a while, the TZ88
    is  new).   We  have an HSC95 with one k.scsi card  (HSC9X-SX),  one
    k.si8 (HSC9X-FA), and 6 k.si (HSC5X-DA) requestors.  My plan  is  to
    put  the  two  TZ's  on  the  HSC95's  k.scsi,  and here is where my
    questions start...


    Rumor #1	"Don't put SCSI tapes on an HSC with disks." 
    --------
        Well, I know  you  don't  put  tapes  on  a requestor with disks
    (can't  mix'n'match), but different requestors on the same HSC?   Is
    this really a problem?  I guess since the tapes are slow compared to
    disks, you would want to put the k.scsi in  a  lower  priority  slot
    than  all your other disks.  But can tape access on the low priority
    requestor really interfere with disk access on the other requestors?
    Any other considerations?

        Oh, this rumor was, I believe,  the primary reason for isolating
    the  Exabytes  on the old HSC50 (which were obtained  and  installed
    long before my time).  It was thought that a problem  with  the  MTI
    interface  or  tapes  could  lead to _disks_ going off-line if there
    were disks on the  same  HSC.   Anyone  have any experience...or war
    stories...along these lines?


    Rumor #2	"Put each SCSI tape on its own SCSI bus."
    --------
        OK, this one I _might_  buy.   I  can  imagine that trying to do
    backups  to two tape drives which are on the same SCSI bus (and same
    k.scsi requestor) could lead to contention for the bus and less than
    optimal performance.  But are separate  busses  a  requirement?   If
    not,  how  badly is the performance affected by putting two tapes on
    one SCSI bus (yes, I know, YMMV).


        To put this in perspective, each night we do a daily incremental
    of all disks to one Exabyte, and a  monthly image of one disk to the
    other  Exabyte.   Twice  a week, we do a weekly incremental  of  all
    disks rather than a daily.  The weeklies run to two tapes,  as  will
    the  dailies  occasionally.   The monthlies (almost) always fit on a
    single Exabyte tape (these are the  2.1(?) GB variety).  The largest
    disks we currently have are RF73's and RA90's.  We have on the order
    of 34 disk volumes that get backed up.

        Any comments would be gratefully received.

    	    -Ken
