In article <52ous9$iie@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>, ard12@eng.cam.ac.uk (A.R. Duell) writes:
|>don@rata.vuw.ac.nz (Don Stokes) writes:
|>
|>>And my collection does include some interesting devices -- various D/A & 
|>>A/D cards, microcoded comms controllers etc...
|>
|>I wasn't aware that there was ever a soft-microcoded comms board (say, 
|>like the KMC11) for the Q-bus, but then I don't know that much about it.

The 68000-based DSV11 Q-bus synch controller has downloadable microcode. The
other (non-Q-bus) synch controllers that use downloadable microcode are the
DSW series, the DSB32 and the DSF32.

I believe that the KMS/KMV series can also have downloadable code (at least
there appear to be customer tools to allow you to develop such code) but I
don't know much about these beasts.

