>>I wasn't aware that there was ever a soft-microcoded comms board (say, 
>>like the KMC11) for the Q-bus, but then I don't know that much about it.

>There's the KMV11 -- I'm not sure what the -11 support for this beast 

Ooops... Never come across that one, although I'm sure it exists. 

>>Of course if you don't mind sticking with 18 bit addressing, you can 
>>always use a DW11-B to link Q-bus stuff up to a Unibus PDP11. DEC never 
>>made a board to go the other way (although I _know_ 3rd parties did). 
>>And, as you say, there is some interesting stuff for the Q-bus - I have a 
>>MINC cabinet full of modules linked up to one of my Unibus systems via 
>>said DW11-B...

>Um, what is the story with the Qbus-UNIBUS perverter found on 11/84s & 
>/94s?

I _thought_ that sat on the PMI rather than the Q-bus, and couldn't (say) 
be used with an 11/23 CPU. Also, does it behave correctly if there are 
(say) DMA devices on both the Unibus and the Processor's Q-bus? It wasn't 
(AFAIK) a supported configuration.


>>don@rata.vuw.ac.nz (Don Stokes) writes:
>>>Seriously, if your first love isn't the care and feeding of hardware at
>>>the component level, Qbus systems do tend to be more practical home
>>>computers, being smaller, quieter and less power hungry than their UNIBUS
>>>brethren.
>>
>>I will disagree with the first bit there - there are other valid reasons 
>>for prefering a Unibus machine at home. The main one (IMHO) is that if 
>>you want to understand how a _real_ processor works at gate level you 
>>could do a lot worse than getting an old Unibus '11, the KM11 test card 
>>set (if you can find it), and single-stepping the machine at a microcycle 
>>level while watching internal CPU signals. 1 hour doing that taught me a 
>>lot more than any book I ever read.

>And if you don't?  Or if having something that runs reliably that you 

Well, get some other machine, then :-). Seriously, a Unibus machine is not
for everyone, but it is quite possible to run one at home. 

And my Unibus machines have been _very_ reliable. Many times I dive into 
the hardware for fun, not because I have to repair it.

>can leave going all the time without driving you batty with the noise or 
>bankrupting you via the power bill is a higher priority than learning 

_Smaller_ Unibus 11's (like the 11/10, which is _almost_ portable) are no 
worse in this respect than, say, a BA23 full of cards.

>about the hardware?  Most people don't acquire computers for the sole 
>purpose of pulling them to bits.

>The pdp11 is a _wonderful_ machine to learn about computer architecture
>on, and you don't need to be handy with a logic probe to do this.  You
>can get significant benefit from a pdp11 without going anywhere near the
>hardware.

Correct. But, you can learn about the software architecture of a PDP11 
equally well on a Unibus or Qbus machine. The Unibus machine offers you 
the additional chance to learn about hardware if you want to.

>Don Stokes, Network Manager, Victoria University of Wellington, New Zealand.

